# Development

The function is a C# class library for .NET 10 (`net10.0`). It needs the
.NET 10 SDK; `scripts/package.sh` also needs `zip`.

## Layout

| Path                                 | Role                                                           |
| ------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `src/Api/Function.cs`                | Lambda entry point (`Api::Api.Function::FunctionHandler`)      |
| `src/Api/Routes.cs`                  | Route table: `"<METHOD> <path>"` to handler                    |
| `src/Api/Router.cs`                  | Routing, JSON responses, body parsing, error handling          |
{%- if values.enableCRUD %}
| `src/Api/Items/ItemRoutes.cs`        | Handlers of the `/items` endpoints                             |
| `src/Api/Items/IItemStore.cs`        | Storage interface of the items                                 |
| `src/Api/Items/DynamoDbItemStore.cs` | Items in the DynamoDB table named by `ITEMS_TABLE`             |
{%- endif %}
| `tests/Api.Tests/`                   | Unit tests (xUnit.net v3), no AWS access needed                |
| `Directory.Build.props`              | Settings of both projects: target framework, warnings, builds  |
| `scripts/package.sh`                 | Writes `build/lambda.zip`                                      |

Package versions are pinned in `src/Api/Api.csproj` and
`tests/Api.Tests/Api.Tests.csproj`. Compiler warnings fail the build.

## Run the tests

```bash
dotnet test Api.slnx --configuration Release
dotnet format Api.slnx --verify-no-changes
```

The second command checks the formatting; without `--verify-no-changes` it
fixes it.

## Build the deployment package

```bash
bash scripts/package.sh
```

This publishes `src/Api` for `linux-x64` (framework-dependent) and writes the
output to `build/lambda.zip`, the file `infra/` deploys. The pipeline does the
same through `.github/actions/build-lambda`.

Release builds are deterministic and the archive has fixed file times,
permissions and order, so unchanged sources give the same archive and
Terraform does not redeploy the function. The pipeline installs the newest
.NET 10 SDK (`10.0.x`); a new SDK release can change the compiled assembly
and with it the archive.

## Implement an endpoint

API Gateway sends every request to the function with the method in
`request.HttpMethod` and the path template in `request.Resource` (for example
`/users/{id}`; the value of `{id}` is `Router.PathParameter(request, "id")`).
`Routes.Create()` in `src/Api/Routes.cs` maps `"<METHOD> <path template>"` to
a handler.

An endpoint requested when the repository was created starts as a stub that
answers `501`. Replace the stub with a handler:

```csharp
public static Dictionary<string, RouteHandler> Create()
{
    // ...
    routes["POST /users"] = CreateUser;

    return routes;
}

private static Task<APIGatewayProxyResponse> CreateUser(APIGatewayProxyRequest request)
{
    var user = Router.ParseObject(request);
    return Task.FromResult(Router.Json(201, user));
}
```

Throw `new HttpException(status, message)` to answer with an error status.
Any other exception is logged and answered with `500`.

To add an endpoint that was not requested at creation, add it to `endpoints`
in `infra/terraform.tfvars.json` as well, so that API Gateway routes it; see
[Operations](operations.md#change-the-configuration).
